<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. O&apos;Keeffe returns for her subsequent annual wellness visit. She completed the health risk assessment before the visit on a large-print form, with the clinic nurse reading the small-print items aloud. Her left ankle sprain from August healed within four weeks with a brace and home exercises, and she no longer uses the cane. That fall is her only fall in the past year; she has had no emergency visits or hospital stays. She continues treat-and-extend aflibercept injections in the left eye with her retina specialist, checks the Amsler grid daily, and reports no new distortion. She takes the AREDS2 supplement daily and no other medicines or over-the-counter products. She paints daily with the task lighting, stand magnifier, and high-contrast labels set up during low-vision rehabilitation. She still drives short local routes by day. Her main concern is staying safe living alone as her vision changes. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Subsequent annual wellness visit</td></tr><tr><td>New concern</td><td>Staying safe at home and on her walks with reduced central vision</td></tr><tr><td>Safety events</td><td>One fall in the past year, with a left ankle sprain, now healed; no emergency visits or hospital stays</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>AREDS2 supplement daily; intravitreal aflibercept given by her retina specialist; no other prescription or over-the-counter products</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Neovascular macular degeneration in the left eye, dry on treat-and-extend injections; intermediate dry macular degeneration in the right eye</td></tr><tr><td>Functional status</td><td>Independent in all activities of daily living; uses magnification and lighting aids for reading, labels, and fine work</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient before the visit and reviewed during the visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Good; worried mainly about her eyesight</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Lives alone by choice; a housekeeper comes on weekdays and her sister calls weekly; painting gives structure and purpose; occasional low days after the fall, none in the past two weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Behavioral</td><td>Never smoker; no alcohol; walks most mornings, now later in the day; simple diet with leafy greens; sleeps about seven hours</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent in bathing, dressing, eating, toileting, and transfers</td></tr><tr><td>Instrumental activities of daily living</td><td>Cooks, shops, and manages money; reads mail and medicine labels with a stand magnifier; drives short routes by day only</td></tr><tr><td>Home safety</td><td>Smoke detectors working; dim hallway and an unmarked single step down to the studio; two loose rugs; no grab bars in the bathroom; no way to call for help if she falls when alone</td></tr><tr><td>Hearing and vision</td><td>Hears well; central vision reduced, worse in the left eye; low-contrast edges and steps are hard to see in flat or dim light</td></tr><tr><td>Falls</td><td>One fall in the past year with injury; not afraid of falling but more careful outdoors</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>No concerns</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

